* Later rounds of uncontested auctions

Phase 4 (NOTES.md #P5). The booklet's "SUBSEQUENT BIDDING BY RESPONDER" and the
rebid framework in `rebids.bml` stop at opener's rebid (and, for a few
auctions, responder's second call). These tables take the most frequent
uncontested auctions one or two calls further, as a plain limit-bidding
scheme: responder, who has shown 6+ (or 10+ after a two-over-one), adds
opener's shown range and bids game with enough for 25--26 in total, invites
with a point or two less, and otherwise stops; opener, asked by an
invitation, accepts with the top of his range. A major fit (eight cards) plays
in the major, everything else in notrump. A forcing new suit by responder at
his second turn is not written (such hands bid the notrump or raise call that
describes their strength), and neither is slam bidding: a game bid ends our
auction. As everywhere in phase 4, every table ends with a pass chain.

#CUT then-accept-14
  (P)
    3N = {prio:1} 13--14 hcp
      #PASTE after-chain
    #PASTE pass-chain
  (any)
    #PASTE pass-chain
#ENDCUT

#CUT then-accept-14-15
  (P)
    3N = {prio:1} 14--15 hcp
      #PASTE after-chain
    #PASTE pass-chain
  (any)
    #PASTE pass-chain
#ENDCUT

#CUT then-accept-major-14-15
  (P)
    4\F = {prio:1} 14--15 hcp
      #PASTE after-chain
    #PASTE pass-chain
  (any)
    #PASTE pass-chain
#ENDCUT

#CUT then-accept-major-14
  (P)
    4\F = {prio:1} 13--14 hcp
      #PASTE after-chain
    3N = 13--14 hcp
      #PASTE after-chain
    #PASTE pass-chain
  (any)
    #PASTE pass-chain
#ENDCUT

#CUT then-game-17
  (P)
    4\F = {prio:1} 17+ hcp
      #PASTE after-chain
    #PASTE pass-chain
  (any)
    #PASTE pass-chain
#ENDCUT

